[38;5;231m⏺[39m I've put a sketch of filter placements in a browser tab. It should have opened by itself; if not, it's at
  [94m]8;id=l2r2h6;http://localhost:49431/?key=9f8cdd27dca0d736a1dfa35dc7cc8c5c1a7590b34259889f84bc521d22c42512\http://localhost:49431/?key=9f8cdd27dca0d736a1dfa35dc7cc8c5c1a7590b34259889f84bc521d22c42512[39m]8;;\. The sketches use a fair
  number of tokens, so tell me if you'd rather stay in the terminal. The sketch files go in [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m, which the
  repo has no [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m for yet, so I'll keep them out of any commit.

  I've read your answer as three needs: failed sign-ins, security changes, and one particular week. "Security changes"
  here means Password changed, Two-factor enabled, and Email change requested. I left out Settings updated because in
  this data it only covers notification preferences.

  Both options on screen have the same filters:
  - quick views for all events, failed sign-ins, and security changes
  - an event type select
  - From and To date fields for picking a week
  - a "Showing X of Y events" line that screen readers announce

  The difference is placement:
  - [1mA: always-visible bar above the table.[0m Everything is one click away, and it stacks into a column on narrow screens.
    It's simpler to build and keeps the diff smaller.
  - [1mB: quick views up front, with event type and dates in a "Filters" popover.[0m Applied filters show as removable tags.
    It's more compact, but it needs an extra click and more code for focus handling.

  I'd go with A: the quick views are what most people want, and A is the smaller diff to review. Which looks right to
  you? You can click one in the browser, but please also reply here.

